#. type: Title #
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:1
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "Docker File Handling Examples"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:10
msgid ""
"If you're not sure how to handle the backup files and how to create the "
"`restore` directory in the docker volume, you can find some examples below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "Restore Inside the Same Stack"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Requires:** console access to the zammad-backup container.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:18
msgid ""
"If you want to restore a backup from the same stack, you just have to create "
"the directory and copy/move the files into it. The following example starts "
"the **zammad-backup** container and copies _all_ .gz files from the backup "
"directory into the restore directory:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "docker compose run --rm zammad-backup bash -c \"mkdir /var/tmp/zammad/restore; cp /var/tmp/zammad/*.gz /var/tmp/zammad/restore -v\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:24
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:47
msgid "Now start the stack to execute the restore process."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:25
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Restore from Another Installation"
msgstr "Инсталација у сопственој режији"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:28
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Requires:** console access to the host system and the zammad-backup container.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:31
msgid ""
"To **obtain** your backup files from another docker compose deployment, one "
"way is to copy it to the host system with `docker compose cp`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:32
#, no-wrap
msgid "docker compose cp zammad-backup:/var/tmp/zammad/ /path/to/your/host/directory/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:38
msgid ""
"In case you are searching for your backup files from a package installation, "
"have a look at the [Backup & Restore (Package)](/en/tutorials/backup-"
"restore) section. You don't need a full dump for restoring your backup."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:41
msgid ""
"To **restore** the backup, place your files in a folder called `restore` on "
"the host system. This folder is mounted temporarily to `/restore` in the "
"backup container. The directory then gets copied to the actual directory:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "docker compose run --rm -v /path/to/your/host/directory:/restore zammad-backup bash -c \"cp -rv /restore /var/tmp/zammad/\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:48
#, no-wrap
msgid "Use a Web GUI"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:51
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Requires:** console access to the host system or Portainer access with the permission to deploy a container.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:53
msgid ""
"This can be useful if you use Portainer to deploy Zammad and have limited "
"access to the host system."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:56
msgid ""
"Our example uses the tool [filebrowser](https://filebrowser.org/), but any "
"similar tool should work too. If you'd like to use such a tool permanently, "
"make sure to provide additional volumes for persistence (e.g. for their "
"database)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (info)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"The steps below cover the restore process by uploading files. To get your backup files in the same way from another\n"
"stack, you can follow steps 1-4 below and simply map the **zammad-backup** volume of your _old_ stack. Then you can\n"
"download the files, stop and remove the filebrowser container and redeploy it, following the steps below.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:66
msgid "Deploy filebrowser"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tabs)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:67
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"   === Via console\n"
"\n"
"   Deploy the container and provide the volume of **zammad-backup** and\n"
"   a port under which you want to access the web UI:\n"
"\n"
"   ``` sh\n"
"   docker run -v zammad-docker-compose_zammad-backup:/srv -p 8089:80 filebrowser/filebrowser\n"
"   ```\n"
"\n"
"   === Via Portainer\n"
"\n"
"   In your Portainer web UI, go to **Containers** in the left menu and\n"
"   click the `Add container` button.\n"
"\n"
"   Add the following information:\n"
"\n"
"   - Name: enter a name which is not already in use.\n"
"   - Image: `filebrowser/filebrowser`\n"
"   - Map additional port: choose a port and map it to port `80` in the container.\n"
"   - Advanced container settings:\n"
"     - Switch to **Volumes** and click the `map additional volume` button.\n"
"     - Enter `/srv` in the container section and select the volume containing `zammad-backup`\n"
"   - Finally, click on **Deploy the container**.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
msgid ""
"After the container is started, go to the web interface by using the IP "
"address and the port you defined."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
msgid "Log in with the default credentials `admin` / `admin`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
msgid "You should now see at least 2 .gz files including a timestamp."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
msgid ""
"Create a **New folder** by using the button on the left side. Name it "
"`restore`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
msgid ""
"Enter this folder and upload your backup files (on the top right corner with "
"the up arrow). If the upload fails, it might be required to change the "
"permissions of the files (e.g. readable for everyone)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:103
msgid ""
"Now start the stack to execute the restore process. After that, you can "
"safely delete the renamed folder and stop the filebrowser."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:1 src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:6
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Backup & Restore (Docker)"
msgstr "Резервна копија и њено враћање"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:9
msgid ""
"This section shows some basics about the backup and restore process for a "
"docker compose based deployment of Zammad."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:15
msgid ""
"If you are familiar with volume based backup and restore procedures in "
"docker, and perhaps already use a different method or tool, then you can "
"keep using it. A backup would typically mean shutting down the stack to "
"ensure all in-memory files get written to disk, then backing up the volume "
"contents, and then starting the stack again. When using such method, you can "
"consider using the [disable-backup-service scenario](/en/reference/docker-"
"compose-scenarios) so that the built-in backup and restore mechanism of "
"Zammad is not activated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:17
msgid ""
"The rest of this page describes the built-in backup and restore mechanism of "
"Zammad's docker compose stack."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:21
msgid ""
"If you're familiar with docker, the sections below include the information "
"you'll need. The [docker file handling](/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling) "
"page covers some examples about how to handle the backup files and to copy "
"it into a docker volume to restore it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:22
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Backup"
msgstr "Zammad"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:26
msgid ""
"By default, a backup is created at each start of the stack as well as at 3 "
"o'clock each night. The backup is stored in the volume of the **zammad-"
"backup** container under `/var/tmp/zammad`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:27
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Restore"
msgstr "Резервна копија и њено враћање"

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:40
msgid "Start the new stack at least once so a Zammad database is available."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:40
#, fuzzy
msgid "Stop the stack."
msgstr "Корак 3: Покретање stack-а"

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:40
msgid ""
"In case you restore to a production stack with activated file system "
"storage, you should purge the content of the directory `/opt/zammad/storage/"
"` inside the volume. The restore process only adds/overwrites files there, "
"no cleanup will take place."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:40
msgid ""
"Copy or move the backup files to `/var/tmp/zammad/restore/` inside the "
"volume of the **zammad-backup** container. Be aware that the restore process "
"always uses the latest backup according to the timestamp of the file name. "
"Only backups from package and docker installations are supported by this "
"built-in backup method. Don't provide the `latest_zammad_*.gz` files because "
"they link to an unknown location for the restore process."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:40
msgid ""
"Start the stack. The restore process is triggered if the `restore` directory "
"is detected and the backup files are in place."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-restore.md:40
msgid ""
"After the restore process has finished, the `restore` directory got renamed. "
"You can safely delete it now."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:1
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "Firewall & SELinux"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:10
msgid ""
"This is just a collection of snippets which might be useful for you. Feel "
"free to skip parts and/or adapt it to your needs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "SELinux"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (info)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:13
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following commands only work on Ubuntu, Debian and CentOS. If you use a\n"
"different distribution, please have a look at their documentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo chcon -Rv --type=httpd_sys_content_t /opt/zammad/public/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:22
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo setsebool httpd_can_network_connect on -P\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:26
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t /opt/zammad/public/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:30
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo restorecon -Rv /opt/zammad/public/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:34
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo chmod -R a+r /opt/zammad/public/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:38
#, no-wrap
msgid "Firewall"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:43
msgid ""
"Ensure to open ports `80` and `443` (TCP & UDP) beside of the ports you "
"need. Below you can find a few examples for different distributions. If you "
"are using a different distribution, please have a look at their "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:46
msgid ""
"Please note that the examples below only cover the distribution’s default "
"firewall. It may not cover your case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tabs)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"===Ubuntu\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo ufw allow 80\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo ufw allow 443\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo ufw reload\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"===Debian\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tabs|info)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We’re covering `nftables` in this part - `iptables` is discouraged\n"
"starting from Debian 10 (Buster). Our example uses the `input` chain, yours\n"
"may be a different one!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tabs)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"Add the following lines to `/etc/nftables.conf` or your specific rule file.\n"
"Ensure to add these lines to your input-chain.\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo tcp dport { http, https } accept\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo udp dport { http, https } accept\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"The result can look like the following. Keep in mind that your environment\n"
"could require different / more rules.\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"table inet filter {\n"
"   chain input {\n"
"      type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;\n"
"      ct state established,related accept\n"
"      tcp dport ssh log accept\n"
"      tcp dport { http, https } accept\n"
"      udp dport { http, https } accept\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"   chain forward {\n"
"      type filter hook forward priority 0; policy accept;\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"   chain output {\n"
"      type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;\n"
"   }\n"
"}\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"To load the rules, run:\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo systemctl reload nftables\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"===CenOS, RHEL, OpenSUSE, SLES\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=http --permanent\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=https --permanent\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo firewall-cmd --reload\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

