#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "Single Sign-on with Kerberos"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:6
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Single Sign-On for Kerberos <Badge type=\"warning\" text=\"on-premise only\"/>"
msgstr "Одељак са значком <Badge type=\"warning\" text=\"прилагођен текст\" />"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:10
msgid ""
"This guide will discuss how to set up single sign-on using Microsoft Active "
"Directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:11
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "Conceptual Overview"
msgstr "Преглед функција"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:16
msgid ""
"Like every other web application out there, Zammad has its own logic for "
"signing users up, storing their passwords, authenticating them, and managing "
"their sessions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:22
msgid ""
"If your IT department keeps its own user identity store (like Active "
"Directory), Zammad's SSO support allows you to leverage that existing auth "
"system so that anyone with an account on your local intranet will 1) "
"automatically have an account in Zammad and 2) be able to log in with a "
"single click."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tip)
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:23
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If you don't have this IT infrastructure but still want one-click\n"
"login, you can use alternatives like Github, Google, Facebook and more.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:28
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Does it Work?"
msgstr "Како то уствари функционише?"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:34
msgid ""
"Once enabled, single sign-on activates an endpoint at `https://"
"your.zammad.host/auth/sso`. When the Zammad server receives a GET request at "
"this endpoint with a valid username in **any one of the following** "
"entities, it creates a new session for that user:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:38
msgid "an `X-Forwarded-User` request header"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:38
msgid "a `REMOTE_USER` web server environment variable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:38
msgid "an `HTTP_REMOTE_USER` web server environment variable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (info)
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:39
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Wait. SSO allows you to sign in with only a username?**\n"
"\n"
"In principle, yes.\n"
"\n"
"**How is that okay?**\n"
"\n"
"In this guide, we configure our web server (Apache) to intercept all\n"
"requests to the `/auth/sso` endpoint. Instead of forwarding them to\n"
"Zammad, Apache initiates a three-sided login process (_Kerberos\n"
"authentication_) between the itself, the user, and the Active\n"
"Directory server.\n"
"\n"
"If Active Directory doesn't recognize the user or their password,\n"
"Zammad never sees the request, and the session is never created.\n"
"\n"
"**What does this all mean?**\n"
"\n"
"It means there are many ways you could set up SSO—you don't need to\n"
